### Page 6 of 16

```text
UDC-23-00049
May 1, 2023
The division of land proposed would not substantively change the density range of the
neighborhood.

The application complies with this review criterion.

2) The application reflects sound planning and urban design principles with respect to existing
and potential future development in the vicinity, including consideration of current and future
streets, open spaces, blocks and lots, and public utilities or facilities. (UDC 2.1.2.d.2)

The proposed plat is in an area with existing infrastructure and no changes to the street or utility
network are proposed. The proposed plat would not change the existing conditions in a way
which would adversely affect the ability of the property to the north to redevelop.

The application complies with this review criterion.

3) The division of land meets the purposes, intent, and all applicable standards in Article 4. (UDC
2.1.2.d.3)

Community Facilities and Fees (4.2):
The proposed subdivision is a replat of land originally platted in 1961. No park fees were
charged with the original plat. When land is being replatted, it is not exempt if it adds lots.
The acreage in the plat is small enough the minimum fee of $250 of Park Fees will be due at
signing of the plat.
Per UDC Section 4.2.5, Table 4-2, the Land Acquisition & Infrastructure fee are payable at
the time of the signing of the final plat.
Per UDC Section 4.2.5, Table 4-2, Enhancement Fees are payable at the issuance of a
building permit for each structure (Residential Enhancement Fee is $400 per dwelling unit).

Transportation Network (4.3):
This plat is occurring within an area of the community which has largely been previously
developed. The plat is limited by pervious development patterns. An exception for block
sizes must be granted (the block the plat is on includes the Golf Course, Cahill Park, and East
High School, but there is nothing this platting action could do to create new connections).

Open Space (4.4):
Open space is required at 750sqft per unit or 8% of the gross development area, whichever
is less. However, due to the nature of this application, plat-level open space is not required.

Required Improvements (4.5):
The infrastructure surrounding this plat is largely already in place. Some minor utility work
may be needed to assure all properties can be serviced, but the plat provides an easement .

Public Safety Fees (4.6):
Public Safety Fees will be due with building permits of new construction on the site.

The application complies with this review criterion, with an exception.

4) Any phasing proposed in the application is clearly indicated and demonstrates a logical and
well-planned development pattern. This may include consideration of amenities and public
```

